// Chapter headings and background written for Joined: Leviticus, Numbers and Deuteronomy.
Object.assign(CHAPTERS,{
"lev.1":{title:"It shall be accepted for him to make atonement for him",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord speaks to Moses out of the tabernacle—The law of the burnt offering: a male without blemish from the herd, offered “of his own voluntary will”—The offerer lays his hand on its head, and it is accepted to make atonement for him—The poor may bring turtledoves or young pigeons, “a sweet savour unto the LORD.”",
 bg:["The Hebrew word for offering, qorban, comes from a root meaning “to draw near”; sacrifice was a way of coming close to God. The burnt offering (Hebrew olah, “that which goes up”) was burned entirely on the altar, a sign of giving oneself wholly to the Lord.","An angel taught Adam that sacrifice “is a similitude of the sacrifice of the Only Begotten of the Father” (Moses 5:7). Laying a hand on the animal’s head pictured an innocent substitute standing in the offerer’s place, as Jesus Christ would for all."]},
"lev.2":{title:"The salt of the covenant of thy God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The meat offering is of fine flour with oil and frankincense—A handful is burned as a memorial, and the rest belongs to the priests—No leaven or honey may be burned on the altar—Every offering is seasoned with “the salt of the covenant”—Firstfruits are offered as green ears of corn dried by the fire.",
 bg:["In the King James Bible “meat” means food in general; this was a grain offering (Hebrew minchah, “gift” or “tribute”), with no animal involved. It let families give the Lord part of their daily bread, and much of it fed the priests who served at the tabernacle.","Salt preserves and does not decay, so it became a symbol of a lasting covenant (2:13). The Lord told Latter-day Saints that those who make an everlasting covenant “are accounted as the salt of the earth” (D&C 101:39)."]},
"lev.3":{title:"A sacrifice of peace offering",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The law of the peace offering, male or female without blemish, from the herd or the flock—The offerer lays his hand on its head, and the blood is sprinkled on the altar—The fat and the kidneys are burned as “the food of the offering made by fire”—“All the fat is the LORD’s”: Israel is to eat neither fat nor blood.",
 bg:["The Hebrew name for this offering, shelamim, is related to shalom, “peace” or “wholeness.” Unlike the burnt offering, most of the meat was eaten by the worshipper’s family in a joyful meal, a picture of peace and fellowship between God and his people.","The fat was considered the richest part, so it was given to the Lord (3:16). The command not to eat blood is explained later: “the life of the flesh is in the blood” (17:11)."]},
"lev.4":{title:"If a soul shall sin through ignorance",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The sin offering for sins done through ignorance—An anointed priest or the whole congregation brings a young bullock, whose blood is sprinkled seven times before the vail—Its body is burned without the camp—A ruler brings a male goat, a common person a female goat or lamb—The priest makes an atonement, “and it shall be forgiven.”",
 bg:["The sin offering (Hebrew chatta’t) dealt with sins committed unknowingly or by mistake. The larger the person’s responsibility, the larger the offering: a priest’s sin affected the whole people, so it required as much as the sin of all Israel.","The bodies of some sin offerings were burned “without the camp” (4:12, 21). The epistle to the Hebrews draws the link: “Wherefore Jesus also, that he might sanctify the people with his own blood, suffered without the gate” (Hebrews 13:11–12)."]},
"lev.5":{title:"He shall confess that he hath sinned",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Sins of silence, uncleanness and rash oaths—The guilty one shall confess and bring a lamb or kid—If he cannot afford a lamb, two turtledoves or pigeons; if not, fine flour—A trespass offering of a ram, with a fifth part added, for trespass in holy things—“He hath certainly trespassed against the LORD.”",
 bg:["The law made room for the poor: whoever could not afford a lamb could bring birds, and whoever could not afford birds could bring a little flour (5:7, 11). No one was too poor to be forgiven.","Confession came before the offering (5:5). The Lord teaches the same principle today: “he will confess them and forsake them” (D&C 58:43)."]},
"lev.6":{title:"The fire shall ever be burning upon the altar",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Whoever deceives or robs his neighbour must restore it in full and add a fifth part, then bring a trespass offering—The law of the burnt offering: the fire on the altar shall never go out—The law of the meat offering, eaten by the priests in the holy place—The law of the sin offering: “it is most holy.”",
 bg:["Sin against a neighbour was also counted as a trespass “against the LORD” (6:2). Forgiveness required more than a sacrifice: the wrongdoer first repaid what he took plus twenty percent (6:4–5), much as Zacchaeus later offered to restore what he had taken (Luke 19:8).","The altar fire, first kindled by the Lord (9:24), was to be kept burning day and night (6:13). It was a constant sign that the way to God stayed open."]},
"lev.7":{title:"The sacrifice of thanksgiving",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The law of the trespass offering, which is most holy—Peace offerings for thanksgiving, for a vow or of free will, with unleavened cakes—Israel shall eat no fat and no blood—The wave breast and heave shoulder are given to Aaron and his sons—These laws the Lord commanded Moses in mount Sinai.",
 bg:["A peace offering could be given simply to say thank you to God (7:12), or to fulfill a promise. The meat had to be eaten within a day or two, so families often shared it with others in a feast.","Portions of many offerings went to the priests, who had no farmland of their own. Paul pointed to this law: “they which minister about holy things live of the things of the temple” (1 Corinthians 9:13)."]},
"lev.8":{title:"Moses brought Aaron and his sons, and washed them",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses gathers the congregation to the door of the tabernacle—Aaron and his sons are washed, clothed in holy garments and anointed with oil—The Urim and Thummim are placed in the breastplate—Blood is put on the right ear, thumb and great toe—The priests remain at the tabernacle seven days to be consecrated.",
 bg:["This chapter carries out the instructions given in Exodus 29. The priesthood given to Aaron and his sons is what Latter-day Saints call the Aaronic Priesthood, restored by John the Baptist in 1829 (D&C 13).","Blood placed on the ear, thumb and toe may suggest that the priest’s hearing, work and walk all belonged to God. The Bible does not explain the symbol, but the same rite is used to cleanse a healed leper in Leviticus 14."]},
"lev.9":{title:"The glory of the LORD appeared unto all the people",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"On the eighth day Aaron offers his first sacrifices, for himself and for the people—“To day the LORD will appear unto you”—Aaron lifts up his hand and blesses the people—The glory of the Lord appears, and fire from before the Lord consumes the offering—The people shout and fall on their faces.",
 bg:["After seven days of consecration, Aaron began his service on the eighth day, a number often linked with new beginnings. He first offered for his own sins and then for the people (9:7); Hebrews 7:27 notes that Jesus, being sinless, needed no offering for himself.","Fire from the Lord showed that he accepted the tabernacle and its priesthood. This was the purpose of the whole tabernacle: that God might dwell among his people and show them his glory."]},
"lev.10":{title:"I will be sanctified in them that come nigh me",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Nadab and Abihu offer “strange fire before the LORD, which he commanded them not,” and fire from the Lord devours them—Aaron held his peace—Priests may not drink wine or strong drink when they serve—They must “put difference between holy and unholy”—Moses is content with Aaron’s explanation about the sin offering.",
 bg:["Nadab and Abihu had seen the God of Israel on Mount Sinai (Exodus 24:9–10), yet they treated holy things carelessly. The Bible does not say exactly what the “strange fire” was; some think the fire or incense was not from the altar, and the command about wine that follows has led some to wonder whether they were drunk.","The lesson was that those who serve God must do so in his way, with reverence. Priests were also to teach Israel the difference between holy and common things (10:10–11)."]},
"lev.11":{title:"Ye shall be holy; for I am holy",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel may eat beasts that part the hoof and chew the cud, but not the camel, coney, hare or swine—Water creatures need fins and scales—Unclean birds and creeping things are listed, though locusts may be eaten—Touching a carcase makes one unclean until the even—“Ye shall be holy; for I am holy.”",
 bg:["These food laws are still kept by many Jews today (called keeping kosher). The Bible gives no reason for most of them; suggestions include health, avoiding pagan practices, or simply setting Israel apart, but no one knows for certain. The stated purpose is holiness: “ye shall be holy; for I am holy” (11:44–45).","Peter quoted this verse to Christians, “Be ye holy; for I am holy” (1 Peter 1:16). In a vision the Lord later taught Peter that the gospel was for all nations and the food laws no longer applied (Acts 10:9–16, 28)."]},
"lev.12":{title:"The days of her purifying",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"After childbirth a mother is unclean for a time and does not enter the sanctuary—A son is circumcised on the eighth day—When her days of purifying are fulfilled, she brings a lamb and a pigeon or turtledove—If she cannot afford a lamb, two turtles or two young pigeons.",
 bg:["Being “unclean” in Leviticus is about ritual readiness to enter the sanctuary, not about sin or shame; childbirth was a blessing. Scholars are not sure why the time was longer after the birth of a daughter.","After Jesus was born, Mary came to the temple and offered “a pair of turtledoves, or two young pigeons” (Luke 2:22–24), the offering allowed for families who could not afford a lamb (12:8)."]},
"lev.13":{title:"He shall cry, Unclean, unclean",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A priest examines risings, scabs and bright spots in the skin—A person may be shut up seven days and examined again—One with the plague of leprosy shall cry, “Unclean, unclean,” and dwell alone without the camp—Leprosy in a garment of wool, linen or skin is washed or burned.",
 bg:["The Hebrew word translated “leprosy” (tsara’at) covered many skin conditions and even mould in cloth and houses; it was probably not always the disease called leprosy today. The priest acted more like an inspector than a doctor, deciding who could take part in worship.","A leper lived apart and warned others away (13:45–46). Jesus reached past this barrier: he “put forth his hand, and touched” a leper and healed him (Mark 1:41)."]},
"lev.14":{title:"The law of the leper in the day of his cleansing",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A healed leper is cleansed with two birds, cedar wood, scarlet and hyssop: one bird is killed, and the living bird is let loose—He washes, shaves and is sprinkled—On the eighth day blood and oil are put on his ear, thumb and toe—The poor may bring a lesser offering—A plague in a house is examined, and the house cleansed or broken down.",
 bg:["This ritual welcomed a healed person back into the camp and to the tabernacle. The living bird, dipped in the blood of the bird that died and set free, has been seen by many readers as a picture of life and freedom made possible through a death.","When Jesus healed a leper, he told him to show himself to the priest “and offer for thy cleansing those things which Moses commanded” (Mark 1:44). Like a consecrated priest (8:23–24), the cleansed person received blood and oil on the ear, thumb and toe."]},
"lev.15":{title:"Thus shall ye separate the children of Israel from their uncleanness",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A man with a running issue is unclean, and what he touches is unclean—He washes, waits seven days and offers two turtledoves or pigeons—Normal bodily issues of men and women bring uncleanness until the even or for seven days—Israel is kept from defiling the tabernacle that is among them.",
 bg:["These laws treat natural bodily discharges as making a person ritually unclean for a time, cured by washing and waiting, and sometimes an offering. This was not sin; it marked the line between ordinary life and the holiness of God’s tabernacle (15:31).","A woman who had suffered an issue of blood for twelve years would have been considered unclean all that time. When she touched Jesus’ clothes in faith, he did not rebuke her but healed her and called her “Daughter” (Mark 5:25–34)."]},
"lev.16":{title:"The goat shall bear upon him all their iniquities",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Aaron may not come within the vail at all times—Once a year, on the Day of Atonement, the high priest enters the most holy place with blood and incense—Lots are cast on two goats: one for the Lord, one for the scapegoat—Aaron confesses Israel’s sins upon the live goat, which bears them into the wilderness—“That ye may be clean from all your sins before the LORD.”",
 bg:["The Day of Atonement (Hebrew Yom Kippur) was the holiest day of Israel’s year. Kippur comes from a root meaning “to cover” or “to atone,” related to the word for the mercy seat (kapporet). The high priest went behind the vail only on this day, sprinkling blood on the mercy seat for the sins of all Israel.","Hebrews 9–10 explains that Jesus Christ is the great High Priest, who entered heaven “by his own blood … having obtained eternal redemption for us” (Hebrews 9:12). Like the scapegoat that carried away the people’s sins (16:21–22), he bore our sins so they could be taken far away."]},
"lev.17":{title:"The life of the flesh is in the blood",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Sacrifices are to be brought to the door of the tabernacle, not offered in the open field—Israel shall no more offer sacrifices to devils—“The life of the flesh is in the blood … it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul”—No one may eat blood, Israelite or stranger.",
 bg:["Verse 11 is the Bible’s clearest explanation of why sacrifices used blood: blood stood for life, and life was given to atone for life. Hebrews later teaches that “without shedding of blood is no remission” (Hebrews 9:22).","Amulek taught that Christ’s “great and last sacrifice” would bring “a stop to the shedding of blood” (Alma 34:13–14). After his resurrection the Savior declared, “ye shall offer up unto me no more the shedding of blood,” but “a broken heart and a contrite spirit” (3 Nephi 9:19–20)."]},
"lev.18":{title:"Which if a man do, he shall live in them",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel shall not follow the doings of Egypt or Canaan—Keep my statutes, “which if a man do, he shall live in them”—Marriage and intimacy between close relatives and other sexual sins are forbidden—Children are not to be given to Molech—The land spues out nations that defile themselves.",
 bg:["This chapter sets moral boundaries for family life, protecting the home and the people within it. The Lord contrasts Israel with the nations around them, whose practices he calls defiling (18:3, 24–28).","Molech was a god worshipped by some neighbouring peoples, possibly with child sacrifice (18:21). The Lord’s law stood firmly against such things, teaching that every person is precious to him."]},
"lev.19":{title:"Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"“Ye shall be holy: for I the LORD your God am holy”—Honour parents and keep sabbaths—Leave the corners of the field and the gleanings for the poor and the stranger—Do not curse the deaf or trip the blind, nor bear a grudge, but “love thy neighbour as thyself”—Rise up before the aged, love the stranger, and keep just weights.",
 bg:["This chapter shows that holiness is not only about the temple but about daily life: how we harvest, do business, speak, and treat the disabled, the elderly and foreigners. The repeated phrase “I am the LORD” reminds Israel why these things matter.","Jesus called “love thy neighbour as thyself” (19:18) the second great commandment (Matthew 22:39), and told the parable of the good Samaritan to show who our neighbour is (Luke 10:25–37). Verse 34 asks Israel to love the stranger the same way, remembering they were once strangers in Egypt."]},
"lev.20":{title:"Ye shall be holy unto me",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Whoever gives his seed to Molech shall be put to death—Israel is not to turn to wizards or familiar spirits—Penalties for cursing parents and for sexual sins—“Sanctify yourselves therefore, and be ye holy”—The Lord has severed Israel from other people, that they should be his.",
 bg:["This chapter gives the penalties for many of the sins listed in chapter 18. Ancient Israel was both a church and a nation, so its law dealt with these offences as crimes; today the Church does not apply these civil penalties.","The heart of the chapter is its call: “ye shall be holy unto me: for I the LORD am holy, and have severed you from other people, that ye should be mine” (20:26). God chose Israel to be different so they could bless the world."]},
"lev.21":{title:"They shall be holy unto their God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Priests are not to defile themselves for the dead, except for close kin—They shall be holy and not profane the name of their God—Rules for the marriage of priests and of the high priest—A son of Aaron with a blemish may eat the holy bread but not approach the altar.",
 bg:["Because priests handled holy things, they lived by stricter rules than other Israelites. The high priest in particular represented the whole people before God.","The requirement that a priest be without blemish pointed to a perfect high priest. Hebrews describes Jesus as “holy, harmless, undefiled, separate from sinners” (Hebrews 7:26)."]},
"lev.22":{title:"Whatsoever hath a blemish, that shall ye not offer",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Priests who are unclean shall not eat of the holy things—Who in a priest’s household may eat them—Offerings must be without blemish to be accepted—A young animal stays with its mother seven days—“I will be hallowed among the children of Israel.”",
 bg:["God asked for the best, not leftovers: a blind, lame or sick animal was not acceptable (22:20–22). Later the prophet Malachi rebuked Israel for offering the lame and the sick (Malachi 1:8).","Peter taught that we were redeemed “with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot” (1 Peter 1:19). Every unblemished offering pointed toward the sinless Son of God."]},
"lev.23":{title:"These are the feasts of the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The sabbath is a holy convocation—The feasts of the Lord: Passover and unleavened bread, the sheaf of firstfruits, and the feast of weeks fifty days later—Leave the corners of the field for the poor—The blowing of trumpets, the day of atonement, and the feast of tabernacles, when Israel dwells in booths.",
 bg:["Israel’s calendar was built around holy days that remembered God’s deliverance and celebrated harvests. The feast of weeks came fifty days after the firstfruits, which is why it was later called Pentecost (Greek for “fiftieth”), the day the Holy Ghost came upon the apostles (Acts 2:1–4).","Several feasts point to Jesus Christ: he was crucified at Passover, and Paul called the risen Christ “the firstfruits of them that slept” (1 Corinthians 15:20). Jesus taught at the feast of tabernacles, inviting, “If any man thirst, let him come unto me, and drink” (John 7:37)."]},
"lev.24":{title:"To cause the lamps to burn continually",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Pure olive oil keeps the lamps burning continually before the Lord—Twelve cakes of shewbread are set on the table every sabbath—The son of an Israelite woman blasphemes the name of the Lord and is stoned—“Eye for eye, tooth for tooth”—One law for the stranger and for Israel.",
 bg:["The twelve loaves of shewbread, one for each tribe, sat in God’s presence as a sign of his covenant (24:5–8). Jesus later called himself “the bread of life” (John 6:35).","“Eye for eye” limited punishment so it would not be greater than the harm done, and it applied equally to stranger and Israelite (24:20–22). Jesus taught a higher law: “resist not evil,” but turn the other cheek (Matthew 5:38–39)."]},
"lev.25":{title:"Proclaim liberty throughout all the land",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Every seventh year the land shall rest—The fiftieth year is a jubile: “proclaim liberty throughout all the land,” and every man returns to his possession and his family—“The land is mine; for ye are strangers and sojourners with me”—Help a poor brother, and take no usury—Israelites are the Lord’s servants and shall not be sold as bondmen.",
 bg:["In the jubilee, land returned to its original family, debts were eased, and Israelites who had sold themselves into service went free. The word jubile comes from the Hebrew yobel, the ram’s horn trumpet blown to announce it. Historians are not sure how fully Israel ever kept it.","Jesus began his ministry in Nazareth by reading Isaiah’s promise to “preach deliverance to the captives” and “the acceptable year of the Lord,” words that echo the jubilee (Luke 4:18–19). Verse 10 is also inscribed on the Liberty Bell in Philadelphia."]},
"lev.26":{title:"I will walk among you, and will be your God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Make no idols, and keep my sabbaths—If ye walk in my statutes, the Lord will send rain in due season, peace in the land, and walk among you—If ye will not hearken, he will send terror, famine and scattering among the nations—If they confess their iniquity, he will remember his covenant with Jacob, Isaac and Abraham.",
 bg:["Like Deuteronomy 28, this chapter sets out the blessings of keeping the covenant and the consequences of breaking it. Israel’s later history, including exile to Assyria and Babylon, was understood as fulfilling these warnings.","Even in scattering, God promised not to cast Israel away or break his covenant with them (26:44). Paul quoted verse 12, “I will dwell in them, and walk in them; and I will be their God” (2 Corinthians 6:16)."]},
"lev.27":{title:"It is holy unto the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"When a man makes a vow, persons are valued in shekels of silver—Animals, houses and fields vowed to the Lord may be redeemed by adding a fifth—A devoted thing is most holy and may not be redeemed—“All the tithe of the land … is the LORD’s: it is holy unto the LORD.”",
 bg:["People sometimes vowed themselves or their property to God, often in gratitude or during a crisis. This chapter set fair values so the promise could be kept by paying silver to the sanctuary.","The tithe, a tenth of the crops and herds, belonged to the Lord (27:30–32). Malachi later promised blessings to those who “bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse” (Malachi 3:10), and the Lord renewed the law of tithing in our day (D&C 119:4)."]},
"num.1":{title:"Take ye the sum of all the congregation",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"In the wilderness of Sinai the Lord commands Moses to number Israel—Every man from twenty years old and upward able to go to war is counted by tribes—A leader from each tribe assists Moses and Aaron—The total is 603,550—The Levites are not numbered but are appointed over the tabernacle.",
 bg:["The English title “Numbers” comes from the censuses in chapters 1 and 26. The Hebrew title, Bemidbar, means “in the wilderness,” which describes the whole book well.","The large totals are debated by scholars. Some read the Hebrew word elef, usually “thousand,” as a clan or military unit, which would make the numbers much smaller; others read it as a true thousand."]},
"num.2":{title:"Every man by his own standard",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord arranges the camp of Israel—Each tribe pitches by its own standard around the tabernacle—Judah, Issachar and Zebulun camp on the east toward the rising sun—Reuben, Ephraim and Dan lead camps on the south, west and north—The Levites camp in the midst, and Israel does as the Lord commands.",
 bg:["The tabernacle stood at the center of the camp, with the tribes arranged around it in an orderly square. The layout taught that the Lord’s house belonged at the heart of Israel’s life.","Judah camped on the east, the side of the tabernacle’s entrance, and led the march (10:14). Judah was also the largest tribe in the census (2:4)."]},
"num.3":{title:"The Levites shall be mine",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The sons of Aaron are named, and Nadab and Abihu’s death is recalled—The tribe of Levi is given to Aaron to serve in the tabernacle—The Lord takes the Levites instead of all the firstborn of Israel—The families of Gershon, Kohath and Merari receive their charges—The extra firstborn are redeemed for five shekels each.",
 bg:["Because the Lord spared Israel’s firstborn at the Passover, they belonged to him (3:13). The Levites served in their place, and the firstborn beyond the Levites’ number were redeemed with money.","Latter-day revelation calls the lesser priesthood “the Priesthood of Aaron, because it was conferred upon Aaron and his seed” (D&C 107:13). Nadab and Abihu’s death is told in Leviticus 10:1–2."]},
"num.4":{title:"Every one according to his service",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Levites from thirty to fifty years old are numbered for service—Aaron and his sons cover the ark and holy furniture before the camp moves—The Kohathites carry the holy things but must not touch them, lest they die—The Gershonites carry the curtains and the Merarites the boards and pillars.",
 bg:["Only the priests could handle and cover the most holy things; the Kohathites then carried them on staves without touching them (4:15). Centuries later Uzzah died when he touched the ark (2 Samuel 6:6–7).","Here Levites serve from age thirty, while 8:24 says twenty-five. Commentators have suggested different explanations, such as a period of training, but the text does not say."]},
"num.5":{title:"They shall confess their sin",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The unclean are put out of the camp—Those who wrong another must confess, repay in full and add a fifth part—Offerings given to the priests are theirs—A wife suspected of unfaithfulness is brought before the priest and drinks the bitter water as a test.",
 bg:["Repentance included confessing and making restitution plus twenty percent (5:7). Zacchaeus offered even more when he met Jesus (Luke 19:8).","Other ancient Near Eastern laws used ordeals for an accused wife; the Code of Hammurabi required her to leap into the river (law 132). In Numbers the outcome was left in the Lord’s hands at the sanctuary."]},
"num.6":{title:"The LORD bless thee, and keep thee",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The law of the Nazarite: men or women may vow to separate themselves unto the Lord—They drink no wine, cut no hair and touch no dead body—Offerings are made when the vow is broken or fulfilled—Aaron and his sons are to bless Israel: The Lord bless thee, and keep thee.",
 bg:["Samson and Samuel were dedicated as Nazarites from birth (Judges 13:5; 1 Samuel 1:11), and John the Baptist was to drink “neither wine nor strong drink” (Luke 1:15).","Two tiny silver scrolls found at Ketef Hinnom in Jerusalem in 1979, dated to about 600 BC, carry a form of the priestly blessing (6:24–26). They are among the oldest known copies of any biblical text."]},
"num.7":{title:"The dedication of the altar",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"When the tabernacle is set up, the princes of Israel bring wagons and oxen for the Levites—Each of the twelve princes brings an identical offering on his own day for the dedication of the altar—The gifts are totaled—Moses hears the voice of the Lord speaking from the mercy seat.",
 bg:["With 89 verses this is the longest chapter in the five books of Moses. Each tribe’s identical gift is listed in full, honoring every tribe equally before the Lord.","The chapter ends with the fulfillment of the Lord’s promise to speak with Moses “from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubims” (Exodus 25:22; Numbers 7:89)."]},
"num.8":{title:"The seven lamps shall give light",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Aaron lights the seven lamps of the golden candlestick—The Levites are cleansed, shaved and their clothes washed—The children of Israel lay their hands upon the Levites, who are offered to the Lord—They serve from twenty-five years old until fifty, then assist their brethren.",
 bg:["The whole congregation laid hands on the Levites (8:10), showing that the Levites served on behalf of all Israel, especially in place of the firstborn (8:16–18).","Levites retiring at fifty still helped “to keep the charge” (8:26), a reminder that older members continue to serve in new ways."]},
"num.9":{title:"At the commandment of the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel keeps the Passover in the wilderness of Sinai—Men defiled by a dead body ask what they should do, and the Lord appoints a Passover one month later—Strangers may keep it too—The cloud covers the tabernacle by day and fire by night, and Israel moves or rests at the commandment of the Lord.",
 bg:["The Passover lamb was not to have any bone broken (9:12; Exodus 12:46). John saw this fulfilled when the soldiers did not break Jesus’s legs (John 19:36).","Whether the cloud stayed “two days, or a month, or a year” (9:22), Israel waited for it. Lehi’s family was similarly guided by the Liahona, which worked “according to the faith and diligence and heed” they gave it (1 Nephi 16:28)."]},
"num.10":{title:"Rise up, LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Two silver trumpets are made to call the assembly, sound the march and remember Israel before God—In the second year the cloud lifts, and Israel leaves Sinai in tribal order—Moses invites Hobab to come and be their eyes in the wilderness—When the ark sets forward Moses prays, Rise up, Lord, and let thine enemies be scattered.",
 bg:["Israel had camped at Sinai for nearly a year since arriving (Exodus 19:1; Numbers 10:11). From here the march toward the promised land begins.","Moses’ words when the ark set out (10:35) are echoed in Psalm 68:1: “Let God arise, let his enemies be scattered.”"]},
"num.11":{title:"Would God that all the LORD’s people were prophets",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The people complain, and fire burns at Taberah—The mixed multitude lusts for flesh and remembers the fish and melons of Egypt—Moses says the burden is too heavy, and seventy elders receive the spirit and prophesy—Moses wishes all the Lord’s people were prophets—Quails come, and a plague strikes at Kibroth-hattaavah.",
 bg:["Taberah means “burning,” and Kibroth-hattaavah means “graves of craving,” names that recall what happened there (11:3, 34).","When Joshua wanted Eldad and Medad stopped, Moses answered, “Enviest thou for my sake?” (11:29). Like Moses, the Lord shares his work with many called servants, and today the Church again has Seventies called to help the Twelve (D&C 107:25, 34)."]},
"num.12":{title:"The man Moses was very meek",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Miriam and Aaron speak against Moses because of his Ethiopian wife—The man Moses is very meek, above all men—The Lord declares he speaks with Moses mouth to mouth—Miriam becomes leprous, and Moses cries, Heal her now, O God—Miriam is shut out of the camp seven days.",
 bg:["The Hebrew calls Moses’ wife a Cushite. Scholars disagree whether this means Zipporah or a second wife from Cush, the region south of Egypt.","Moses did not defend himself but prayed for his sister (12:13). Hebrews 3:5 quotes the Lord’s words that Moses was “faithful in all his house” (12:7)."]},
"num.13":{title:"We were in our own sight as grasshoppers",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses sends twelve men, one from each tribe, to search the land of Canaan—Oshea is renamed Jehoshua—The spies carry back a cluster of grapes from Eshcol on a staff between two—Caleb says, Let us go up at once—The others bring an evil report of giants and walled cities.",
 bg:["Jehoshua (Joshua) means “the Lord is salvation” (13:16). It is the same name that comes into Greek as Jesus.","Eshcol means “cluster,” named for the huge bunch of grapes (13:24). Hebron, where the spies saw the sons of Anak, later became Caleb’s inheritance (Joshua 14:13–14)."]},
"num.14":{title:"The LORD is with us: fear them not",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The people weep and want a captain to lead them back to Egypt—Joshua and Caleb plead, The Lord is with us: fear them not—Moses intercedes, and the Lord pardons, but that generation will die in the wilderness—Israel will wander forty years, a year for each day—The people go up without the Lord and are defeated.",
 bg:["Joshua and Caleb saw the same giants but trusted the Lord (14:6–9). Caleb “followed me fully” (14:24), and at eighty-five he was still ready to take his mountain (Joshua 14:10–12).","Hebrews 3:17–19 teaches that this generation “could not enter in because of unbelief.” Latter-day revelation adds that Israel hardened their hearts and could not endure the Lord’s presence (D&C 84:23–25)."]},
"num.15":{title:"Remember all the commandments of the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Laws are given for offerings when Israel comes into the land—One law applies to Israel and the stranger—Sins of ignorance can be atoned for, but presumptuous sin despises the word of the Lord—A man gathering sticks on the Sabbath is stoned—Israel is to wear fringes with a ribband of blue to remember the commandments.",
 bg:["Right after the sentence of forty years, the Lord speaks of “when ye be come into the land” (15:2), a quiet promise that Israel’s children would still arrive.","These fringes are the tzitzit still worn by many observant Jews. Jesus’s critics made theirs large for show (Matthew 23:5), and a woman was healed by touching the “hem of his garment” (Matthew 9:20)."]},
"num.16":{title:"Ye take too much upon you",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Korah, Dathan, Abiram and 250 princes rise against Moses and Aaron—Moses asks the Levites, Seek ye the priesthood also?—The earth swallows the rebels, and fire consumes the 250 who offered incense—Their censers become a covering for the altar—Aaron stands between the dead and the living, and the plague is stayed.",
 bg:["Korah was a Levite who already served in the tabernacle but wanted the priesthood (16:9–10). “No man taketh this honour unto himself, but he that is called of God, as was Aaron” (Hebrews 5:4).","Korah’s children did not die (26:11). Their descendants, “the sons of Korah,” are named in the titles of several psalms, such as Psalms 42 and 84."]},
"num.17":{title:"The rod of Aaron was budded",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Each tribe’s prince brings a rod with his name, and Aaron’s name is written on the rod of Levi—The rods are laid before the testimony—Aaron’s rod buds, blossoms and yields almonds—It is kept as a token against the rebels—The people fear to come near the tabernacle.",
 bg:["This sign settled the question raised by Korah’s rebellion: the Lord himself chooses who holds the priesthood. Hebrews 9:4 lists “Aaron’s rod that budded” among the items kept with the ark.","Almond trees are among the first to blossom in the land of Israel. The Hebrew word for almond sounds like the word for “watching,” a wordplay used in Jeremiah 1:11–12."]},
"num.18":{title:"I am thy part and thine inheritance",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Aaron and his sons bear the charge of the sanctuary, helped by the Levites—The priests receive the holy offerings, firstfruits and firstborn by a covenant of salt—The priests have no land, for the Lord is their inheritance—The Levites receive the tithe and give a tenth of it to the Lord.",
 bg:["Salt preserves and does not decay, so a “covenant of salt” (18:19) meant a lasting covenant (compare Leviticus 2:13; 2 Chronicles 13:5).","The Levites who received Israel’s tithes paid tithing on them too (18:26). The law of tithing applied to those who served full time as well."]},
"num.19":{title:"A red heifer without spot",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A red heifer without blemish is slain and burned outside the camp—Cedar, hyssop and scarlet are cast into the fire—Its ashes are kept to make water of separation—Whoever touches a dead body is unclean seven days and must be sprinkled on the third and seventh days.",
 bg:["Hebrews 9:13–14 compares “the ashes of an heifer sprinkling the unclean” with the far greater cleansing of “the blood of Christ.”","The heifer was killed “without the camp” (19:3). Hebrews 13:12 notes that Jesus also “suffered without the gate” to sanctify his people."]},
"num.20":{title:"Hear now, ye rebels",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Miriam dies at Kadesh, and the people chide Moses for lack of water—The Lord tells Moses to speak to the rock, but he says, Hear now, ye rebels, and smites it twice—Moses and Aaron will not bring Israel into the land—Edom refuses passage—Aaron dies on mount Hor, and Eleazar receives his garments.",
 bg:["Meribah means “strife.” Psalm 106:32–33 says the people provoked Moses so that “he spake unadvisedly with his lips.” Earlier the Lord had told him to smite the rock (Exodus 17:6), but this time to speak to it.","Paul taught that the rock that gave Israel water “was Christ” (1 Corinthians 10:4). Edom descended from Esau, which is why Moses wrote, “Thus saith thy brother Israel” (20:14)."]},
"num.21":{title:"When he beheld the serpent of brass, he lived",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel defeats king Arad at Hormah—The people speak against God and Moses, and fiery serpents bite them—Moses makes a serpent of brass on a pole, and all who look upon it live—Israel sings to the well at Beer—Sihon king of the Amorites and Og king of Bashan are defeated.",
 bg:["Jesus said, “As Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of man be lifted up” (John 3:14–15). Book of Mormon prophets taught that many would not look “because of the simpleness of the way” (1 Nephi 17:41; 2 Nephi 25:20; Alma 33:19–22; Helaman 8:14–15).","Israel later burned incense to this brass serpent, so King Hezekiah broke it in pieces and called it Nehushtan (2 Kings 18:4). The “book of the wars of the Lord” quoted in 21:14 has not survived."]},
"num.22":{title:"The LORD opened the mouth of the ass",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Balak king of Moab fears Israel and sends rewards to Balaam to curse them—God forbids Balaam to go, and Balaam says he cannot go beyond the word of the Lord—Balak sends greater princes, and Balaam goes—The angel of the Lord blocks the way, and the ass speaks—Balaam promises to speak only the word God gives.",
 bg:["Balaam said the right words (22:18) but kept seeking permission for what he wanted. Peter warns of those who follow “the way of Balaam,” who “loved the wages of unrighteousness” (2 Peter 2:15–16).","An inscription found in 1967 at Deir Alla in Jordan, dated by scholars to the 800s or 700s BC, tells of a seer named “Balaam son of Beor,” showing he was remembered outside the Bible."]},
"num.23":{title:"How shall I curse, whom God hath not cursed?",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Balaam has Balak build seven altars, and the Lord puts a word in his mouth—How shall I curse, whom God hath not cursed?—Balak takes him to Pisgah, and Balaam blesses Israel again—God is not a man, that he should lie—Balak tries a third place, the top of Peor.",
 bg:["Balaam’s words “God is not a man, that he should lie” (23:19) teach that the Lord keeps his promises. Balak moved Balaam from place to place, but changing the view could not change God’s word.","Samuel Morse sent the first long-distance telegraph message in 1844 using words from 23:23: “What hath God wrought.”"]},
"num.24":{title:"There shall come a Star out of Jacob",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Spirit of God comes upon Balaam, and he says, How goodly are thy tents, O Jacob—Blessed is he that blesseth thee—Balak’s anger is kindled—Balaam foretells a Star out of Jacob and a Sceptre out of Israel—He prophesies concerning Edom, Amalek, the Kenites and Asshur.",
 bg:["Many Jews and Christians have read the “Star out of Jacob” and “Sceptre” (24:17) as a prophecy of a coming king or Messiah. Christians connect it with Jesus, whose star the wise men followed (Matthew 2:2).","“How goodly are thy tents, O Jacob” (24:5) is still recited in Jewish morning prayers. Later Balaam counseled Midian to lead Israel into sin (31:16; Revelation 2:14)."]},
"num.25":{title:"My covenant of peace",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"At Shittim Israel commits whoredom with the daughters of Moab and joins Baal-peor—The Lord’s anger is kindled, and the leaders are punished—Phinehas slays an Israelite and a Midianite woman, and the plague is stayed—Phinehas receives a covenant of an everlasting priesthood—Israel is told to vex the Midianites.",
 bg:["Balaam could not curse Israel, but his counsel led Israel to curse itself through sin (31:16; Revelation 2:14). Paul used this event as a warning (1 Corinthians 10:8), giving the number as 23,000 rather than 24,000.","Psalm 106:28–31 recalls Baal-peor and says Phinehas’s act “was counted unto him for righteousness.”"]},
"num.26":{title:"Save Caleb the son of Jephunneh, and Joshua",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"After the plague the Lord commands a new census in the plains of Moab—Each tribe is numbered by families, and the total is 601,730—The land is to be divided by lot according to the size of each tribe—The Levites are numbered—None of the first census remain except Caleb and Joshua.",
 bg:["This census counts a new generation nearly forty years later. The total is close to the first (1:46), but some tribes changed greatly; Simeon fell from 59,300 to 22,200 (1:23; 26:14).","As in chapter 1, scholars debate whether the Hebrew elef means “thousand” or a smaller clan or military unit. Either way, the point is clear: the Lord kept his word that only Caleb and Joshua would remain (26:65)."]},
"num.27":{title:"The daughters of Zelophehad speak right",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The five daughters of Zelophehad ask for their father’s inheritance—Moses brings their cause before the Lord, who says they speak right—Laws of inheritance are given—Moses is shown the land from mount Abarim—Joshua, a man in whom is the spirit, is set apart by the laying on of hands.",
 bg:["The daughters respectfully brought a question no law had yet answered, and the Lord ruled in their favor (27:7). They later received their land (Joshua 17:3–6).","Moses laid his hands on Joshua and gave him a charge before the congregation (27:23). Deuteronomy 34:9 says Joshua was “full of the spirit of wisdom; for Moses had laid his hands upon him.”"]},
"num.28":{title:"A continual burnt offering",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel is to offer the Lord’s offerings in their due season—Two lambs are offered daily, morning and evening, as a continual burnt offering—Extra offerings are given for the Sabbath and the new moon—Offerings are listed for the Passover, the feast of unleavened bread and the day of firstfruits.",
 bg:["The daily morning and evening lamb (28:3–4), called the “continual” or tamid offering, kept Israel’s worship going every day, not just on holy days.","The day of firstfruits “after your weeks be out” (28:26) was the feast of weeks, later called Pentecost, when the Holy Ghost came upon the apostles (Acts 2:1–4)."]},
"num.29":{title:"An holy convocation",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The seventh month begins with a day of blowing the trumpets—On the tenth day Israel afflicts their souls—From the fifteenth day a seven-day feast is kept, with fewer bullocks offered each day—An eighth day closes the feast—These offerings are beside vows and freewill offerings.",
 bg:["These holy days became the Jewish New Year (Rosh Hashanah), the Day of Atonement (Yom Kippur, see Leviticus 16) and the Feast of Tabernacles (Sukkot), still observed today.","During the seven-day feast the bullocks decrease from thirteen to seven, seventy in all. Jewish tradition later linked this number to the seventy nations of the world."]},
"num.30":{title:"If a man vow a vow unto the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A man who vows to the Lord shall not break his word—A young woman’s vow may be disallowed by her father on the day he hears it—A wife’s vow may be confirmed or made void by her husband—The vow of a widow or divorced woman stands.",
 bg:["The principle is that promises to God must be kept (30:2). “When thou vowest a vow unto God, defer not to pay it” (Ecclesiastes 5:4).","These laws reflect a household where fathers and husbands were responsible for the family’s obligations. Jesus later taught his disciples to make their simple yes or no enough (Matthew 5:33–37)."]},
"num.31":{title:"Avenge the children of Israel of the Midianites",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord commands war against Midian, with a thousand men from each tribe—The five kings of Midian and Balaam are slain—Moses is angry that the women who followed Balaam’s counsel were spared—The soldiers are purified—The spoil is divided, and the officers offer gold because not one man was lost.",
 bg:["This chapter explains how Balaam died (31:8) and how he caused Israel’s sin at Peor “through the counsel of Balaam” (31:16), which Revelation 2:14 also describes. Readers have long found the war’s harshness difficult, and the text gives no further explanation.","The spoil was divided between the soldiers and the congregation, with a share for the Lord (31:27–29). David later made a similar rule (1 Samuel 30:24–25)."]},
"num.32":{title:"Be sure your sin will find you out",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Reuben and Gad ask for the cattle land east of Jordan—Moses fears they will discourage Israel as the spies did—They promise to go armed before their brethren until the land is won—Moses warns, Be sure your sin will find you out—Gad, Reuben and half of Manasseh receive Gilead and build cities.",
 bg:["Gilead and the land east of the Jordan had good pastureland for herds. Moses accepted the request once these tribes promised to help the others first.","They kept their promise, and Joshua later praised them for not leaving their brethren (Joshua 22:1–4)."]},
"num.33":{title:"These are the journeys of the children of Israel",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses writes the journeys of Israel from Rameses to the plains of Moab—The camps are listed, including the Red sea, Sinai and Kadesh—Aaron dies on mount Hor at 123 years old—Israel is told to drive out the inhabitants and destroy their idols—Those left will be pricks in their eyes and thorns in their sides.",
 bg:["The list names about forty stopping places. Many of them cannot be located today, and scholars disagree about the route.","The warning about “thorns in your sides” (33:55) came true when Israel did not drive out the Canaanites (Judges 2:3). Nephi likewise recounted how the Lord led Israel through the wilderness (1 Nephi 17:23–42)."]},
"num.34":{title:"This is the land which ye shall inherit",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord sets the borders of Canaan, from the wilderness of Zin to the great sea and the entrance of Hamath—The eastern border follows the Jordan to the salt sea—The land is for nine and a half tribes—Eleazar, Joshua and a prince from each tribe, including Caleb, are chosen to divide it.",
 bg:["The “great sea” is the Mediterranean and the “salt sea” the Dead Sea. The “river of Egypt” is usually identified with the Wadi el-Arish in the northern Sinai, though some scholars suggest other locations.","Caleb, one of the two faithful spies, was named to help divide the land he had searched forty years before (34:19)."]},
"num.35":{title:"Cities of refuge",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Levites receive forty-eight cities with pasture lands—Six of them are cities of refuge for one who kills another unawares—A murderer is put to death, but the congregation judges the slayer—The slayer stays in the city until the death of the high priest—One witness is not enough, for blood defiles the land.",
 bg:["The six cities of refuge, three on each side of the Jordan, are named in Joshua 20:7–8. They protected someone who killed by accident from revenge until a fair judgment could be made.","Requiring more than one witness (35:30) became a lasting principle (Deuteronomy 17:6; 2 Corinthians 13:1). Some Christians see the slayer’s freedom at the high priest’s death as a picture of Christ, though the text itself does not say so."]},
"num.36":{title:"Let them marry to whom they think best",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The heads of Manasseh fear the daughters of Zelophehad’s land will pass to another tribe if they marry outside it—Moses commands that they marry whom they think best, but within their father’s tribe—No inheritance shall move from tribe to tribe—The daughters marry their father’s brothers’ sons.",
 bg:["In the year of jubile land returned to its family (Leviticus 25:10), but land inherited by a daughter would have stayed with her husband’s tribe (36:4). This ruling kept each tribe’s inheritance intact.","Numbers ends with Israel camped in the plains of Moab by Jericho (36:13). Deuteronomy records Moses’ final words in the same place (Deuteronomy 1:1–5)."]},
"deu.1":{title:"Ye have dwelt long enough in this mount",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"In the fortieth year Moses speaks to all Israel in the land of Moab—He recalls appointing wise men as judges: “ye shall hear the small as well as the great”—The twelve spies search out the land, but the people murmur and will not go up—That generation may not enter, save Caleb and Joshua—Israel fights without the Lord and is chased “as bees do.”",
 bg:["The name Deuteronomy comes from the Greek translation of 17:18, where the king is to write “a copy of this law”; the Greek means “second law” or “repetition of the law.” Most of the book is Moses’ farewell sermons on the plains of Moab before Israel crosses the Jordan.","Jewish and Christian tradition holds that Moses wrote the book. Many scholars date it later and connect it with the “book of the law” found in the temple in King Josiah’s day (2 Kings 22:8); views differ. The spies story Moses retells here is told in Numbers 13–14."]},
"deu.2":{title:"Ye have compassed this mountain long enough",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord tells Israel to turn northward—They are not to meddle with Edom, Moab or Ammon, whose lands the Lord has given them—Thirty-eight years pass until the men of war have died—Sihon king of Heshbon refuses to let Israel pass, and the Lord delivers him into their hand.",
 bg:["Edom descended from Esau, Jacob’s brother, and Moab and Ammon from Abraham’s nephew Lot (Genesis 19:36–38). That is why the Lord calls them Israel’s brethren and protects their lands.","The defeat of Sihon is also told in Numbers 21:21–24. The giants mentioned here, the Emims, Anakims and Zamzummims, were remembered as tall peoples who lived in the land before."]},
"deu.3":{title:"Let it suffice thee",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel defeats Og king of Bashan and takes threescore cities—Og’s iron bedstead is nine cubits long—The land east of Jordan is given to Reuben, Gad and half of Manasseh, whose men must still help their brethren—Moses pleads to go over Jordan, but the Lord says, “Let it suffice thee”—Moses is to view the land from Pisgah and charge Joshua.",
 bg:["Nine cubits is about thirteen feet. The story of Og’s defeat is also in Numbers 21:33–35.","The Lord had told Moses and Aaron they would not bring Israel into the land because of what happened at the waters of Meribah (Numbers 20:12). Moses accepts the answer and turns to preparing Joshua."]},
"deu.4":{title:"If thou seek him with all thy heart",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel is not to add to or diminish the Lord’s word—Keeping the law is their wisdom before the nations—They heard God’s voice at Horeb but saw no similitude, so they must make no graven image—If scattered, they will find the Lord when they seek him “with all thy heart”—Moses sets apart three cities of refuge east of Jordan.",
 bg:["Verses 27–31 foretell that Israel would be scattered among the nations and, “in the latter days,” would turn to the Lord again. Latter-day Saints see the gathering of Israel as part of the fulfillment.","“The Lord thy God is a consuming fire” (4:24) is quoted in Hebrews 12:29. Cities of refuge protected a person who killed someone by accident (Numbers 35:9–15)."]},
"deu.5":{title:"Hear, O Israel, the statutes and judgments",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The covenant at Horeb was made “with us, even us, who are all of us here alive this day”—Moses repeats the Ten Commandments—The people, afraid of the fire, ask Moses to hear God for them—The Lord says, “O that there were such an heart in them”—Israel is to turn not aside to the right hand or to the left.",
 bg:["The Ten Commandments also appear in Exodus 20. In Exodus the Sabbath recalls the Creation (Exodus 20:11); here it recalls the Lord bringing Israel out of slavery in Egypt (5:15).","Most of the people listening had been children or not yet born at Sinai. Moses teaches that the covenant belongs to each new generation, just as each person today makes covenants personally."]},
"deu.6":{title:"Hear, O Israel: The LORD our God is one LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Hear, O Israel: love the Lord “with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy might”—Teach these words diligently to thy children and write them on thy gates—Beware lest thou forget the Lord in a land of plenty—Ye shall not tempt the Lord—When thy son asks, tell him how the Lord brought you out of Egypt.",
 bg:["Verses 4–9 are called the Shema, from the Hebrew word for “hear.” Jesus called it the first and great commandment (Mark 12:29–30), and faithful Jews still recite it daily.","When Satan tempted Jesus, the Savior answered from this chapter: “Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God” (6:16; Matthew 4:7) and “Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God, and him only shalt thou serve” (see 6:13; Matthew 4:10)."]},
"deu.7":{title:"An holy people unto the LORD thy God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel is to make no covenant or marriages with the nations of Canaan and destroy their idols—Thou art an holy people, chosen not for thy numbers but because the Lord loved you—He keeps covenant and mercy with them that love him—The Lord will drive out the nations “by little and little.”",
 bg:["The Lord explains that he did not choose Israel because they were many, “for ye were the fewest of all people” (7:7), but because of his love and his oath to their fathers.","Marriage outside the covenant was forbidden because it would “turn away thy son from following me” (7:4). Solomon’s foreign wives later turned his heart to other gods (1 Kings 11:1–4)."]},
"deu.8":{title:"Man doth not live by bread only",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord led Israel forty years in the wilderness to humble and prove them—He fed them with manna, that they might know man lives “by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of the LORD”—Their raiment waxed not old—When ye are full, beware lest ye say, “My power … hath gotten me this wealth”—It is the Lord who gives power to get wealth.",
 bg:["Jesus quoted 8:3 when Satan tempted him to turn stones into bread (Matthew 4:4). The manna story is in Exodus 16.","The warning against forgetting God in prosperity matches the pattern the Book of Mormon describes, where the Lord prospers his people and then they harden their hearts and forget him (Helaman 12:1–2)."]},
"deu.9":{title:"Not for thy righteousness",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord will go before Israel as a consuming fire—Israel receives the land not for its righteousness but because of the wickedness of the nations and the oath to the fathers—Moses recalls the golden calf and breaking the tables of stone—He fell down before the Lord forty days and nights to plead for the people and for Aaron.",
 bg:["The story of the golden calf and Moses’ pleading is told in Exodus 32. Moses reminds Israel that they had been “rebellious against the LORD from the day that I knew you” (9:24).","Moses’ prayer (9:26–29) asks the Lord to remember Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. He relies on God’s covenant and mercy, not the people’s goodness."]},
"deu.10":{title:"What doth the LORD thy God require of thee",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses hews two new tables and places them in an ark of shittim wood—The tribe of Levi is separated to bear the ark—What doth the Lord require, but to fear, love and serve him—Circumcise the foreskin of your heart—Love the stranger, for ye were strangers in Egypt.",
 bg:["Compare 10:12 with Micah 6:8: “What doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?”","A circumcised heart means a heart truly committed to God. Paul taught that real circumcision “is that of the heart, in the spirit” (Romans 2:29)."]},
"deu.11":{title:"I set before you this day a blessing and a curse",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel has seen the Lord’s mighty acts in Egypt and the wilderness—Canaan drinks “the rain of heaven,” and the Lord will send the first and latter rain if they obey—Lay up these words in your heart and teach them to your children—A blessing and a curse are set before them, to be proclaimed on mounts Gerizim and Ebal.",
 bg:["Verses 13–21 are recited by Jews together with the Shema of 6:4–9. Egypt was watered from the Nile, but the hills of Canaan depended on seasonal rain, a daily reminder to trust the Lord.","Dathan and Abiram (11:6) rebelled against Moses, and the earth swallowed them (Numbers 16:31–33). The ceremony on Gerizim and Ebal is described in chapter 27."]},
"deu.12":{title:"The place which the LORD your God shall choose",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel is to destroy the places where the Canaanites served their gods—Offerings and tithes must be brought to “the place which the LORD your God shall choose … to put his name there”—Meat may be eaten anywhere, but not the blood, “for the blood is the life”—Do not inquire after the gods of the nations—Add not to the Lord’s commands, nor diminish from them.",
 bg:["“The place” the Lord would choose came to be Jerusalem, where Solomon built the temple, of which the Lord said, “My name shall be there” (1 Kings 8:29). The phrase appears many times in Deuteronomy.","The command not to eat blood is also in Leviticus 17:10–14. Verse 8 warns against doing “whatsoever is right in his own eyes,” a phrase that describes Israel’s later troubles in Judges 21:25."]},
"deu.13":{title:"The LORD your God proveth you",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A prophet or dreamer who gives a sign but says, “Let us go after other gods,” must not be followed—The Lord is proving whether Israel loves him with all their heart—Even a brother, child, wife or friend who entices to idolatry must not be spared—A city that turns to other gods is to be utterly destroyed.",
 bg:["A sign or wonder alone does not prove a prophet true; the test is whether he leads people to the true God. Jesus warned that false prophets would “shew great signs and wonders” (Matthew 24:24).","The severe penalties show how seriously idolatry threatened Israel’s covenant with the Lord, since it was the sin that would bring scattering (4:25–27)."]},
"deu.14":{title:"Ye are the children of the LORD your God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel, a holy and “peculiar people,” is not to cut themselves for the dead—The clean and unclean beasts, fish and birds are listed—Thou shalt truly tithe all thine increase and eat it before the Lord—Tithe may be turned into money if the place is far—Every third year the tithe is shared with the Levite, the stranger, the fatherless and the widow.",
 bg:["“Peculiar” in the King James Bible means a special treasure belonging to God, not strange. Peter calls the Saints “a peculiar people” (1 Peter 2:9).","In Acts 10 the Lord taught Peter through a vision that the old rules of clean and unclean food no longer divided Jews from Gentiles. Latter-day Saints pay tithing under the law revealed in D&C 119."]},
"deu.15":{title:"Thou shalt open thine hand wide",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Every seventh year creditors release debts owed by their brethren—Do not harden thine heart toward the poor, but “open thine hand wide unto him”—A Hebrew servant goes free in the seventh year and is not sent away empty—A servant who chooses to stay has his ear bored with an aul—Firstling males are sanctified to the Lord.",
 bg:["Jesus echoed 15:11, “the poor shall never cease out of the land,” when he said, “Ye have the poor with you always” (Mark 14:7). The verse is a call to keep giving, not an excuse to stop.","Verse 4 hopes there will be “no poor among you.” The people of Enoch had “no poor among them” (Moses 7:18), and after Christ’s visit the Nephites had “all things common,” so “there were not rich and poor” (4 Nephi 1:3)."]},
"deu.16":{title:"Every man shall give as he is able",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Keep the Passover in the month Abib with unleavened bread, “the bread of affliction”—Keep the feast of weeks seven weeks later with rejoicing—Keep the feast of tabernacles seven days after harvest—Three times a year all males appear before the Lord, giving as they are able—Judges must not take a gift, “for a gift doth blind the eyes of the wise.”",
 bg:["The feast of weeks came fifty days after Passover and was later called Pentecost, the day the Holy Ghost was poured out on the disciples (Acts 2:1–4). Jesus was crucified at Passover time.","These three feasts brought Israel together at the place the Lord chose. Rejoicing with family, servants, Levites, strangers, orphans and widows was part of the command (16:11, 14)."]},
"deu.17":{title:"A copy of this law in a book",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Blemished animals may not be sacrificed—An idolater is put to death only “at the mouth of two witnesses, or three”—Hard cases go to the priests and the judge—A king must be chosen by the Lord and must not multiply horses, wives or gold—He must write a copy of this law and read it all his days.",
 bg:["The law of witnesses is repeated in the New Testament (2 Corinthians 13:1) and in latter-day scripture (D&C 6:28). The Greek words for “copy of this law” in 17:18 gave the book its name, Deuteronomy.","Solomon later did the very things the king was warned against, gathering many horses, much gold and many wives, and his heart was turned away (1 Kings 10:14–29; 11:1–4)."]},
"deu.18":{title:"The LORD thy God will raise up unto thee a Prophet",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"The Levites have no inheritance; the Lord is their inheritance—Israel must not use divination, witchcraft or necromancy—“Thou shalt be perfect with the LORD thy God”—The Lord will raise up a Prophet like unto Moses, and all must hearken to him—A prophet whose words do not come to pass has spoken presumptuously.",
 bg:["Peter taught that Jesus Christ is the Prophet like unto Moses (Acts 3:22–23). Nephi taught the same (1 Nephi 22:20–21), and the risen Savior said, “Behold, I am he of whom Moses spake” (3 Nephi 20:23).","The angel Moroni quoted Acts 3:22–23 to Joseph Smith and said this prophet was Christ (Joseph Smith—History 1:40)."]},
"deu.19":{title:"That innocent blood be not shed",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Three cities of refuge are set apart in the land for one who kills his neighbour unawares—A deliberate murderer is not protected—Remove not thy neighbour’s landmark—At least two or three witnesses are needed, and a false witness receives the punishment he intended—Life for life, eye for eye, tooth for tooth.",
 bg:["Jesus referred to the rule of witnesses in 19:15 when teaching how to resolve offenses (Matthew 18:16). He also quoted “eye for eye, tooth for tooth” and taught a higher law: “resist not evil” (Matthew 5:38–39).","The cities of refuge were spread through the land so that anyone could reach one quickly (19:3). Joshua 20 names the cities on both sides of the Jordan."]},
"deu.20":{title:"The LORD your God is he that goeth with you",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Israel need not fear horses and chariots, for the Lord goes with them—Men with a new house, a new vineyard or a new bride, and the fearful, may return home—Proclaim peace to a city before attacking it—The nations of Canaan are to be utterly destroyed—Do not cut down fruit trees during a siege.",
 bg:["Gideon followed the rule of 20:8 when he sent home everyone who was “fearful and afraid” (Judges 7:3).","The command to spare fruit trees (20:19) is one of several laws in Deuteronomy that care for the land and for future generations, not just the present battle."]},
"deu.21":{title:"He that is hanged is accursed of God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"When a murderer is unknown, the elders of the nearest city wash their hands over a heifer and declare their innocence—A captive woman taken as wife must be treated with respect—The firstborn receives a double portion even if his mother is the less loved wife—A stubborn and rebellious son is brought to the elders—A hanged body must be buried the same day.",
 bg:["Paul applied 21:23 to Jesus: “Christ hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us: for it is written, Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree” (Galatians 3:13).","The same law is why the Jews asked that the bodies be taken down from the crosses before the Sabbath (John 19:31). The double portion for the firstborn appears again in Elisha’s request (2 Kings 2:9)."]},
"deu.22":{title:"Thou mayest not hide thyself",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Return thy brother’s lost ox, sheep or garment, and help lift his fallen animal—Let the mother bird go when taking young from a nest—Build a battlement on thy roof—Do not mix seeds, animals at the plow, or wool and linen—Make fringes on thy garments—Laws protect a wrongly accused wife and punish adultery and rape.",
 bg:["A battlement was a low wall around a flat roof, where people often worked or rested. The law made the owner responsible for keeping others safe.","The fringes of 22:12 are also commanded in Numbers 15:38–39, to help Israel “remember all the commandments of the LORD.” A sick woman touched the hem of Jesus’ garment and was healed (Matthew 9:20)."]},
"deu.23":{title:"The LORD thy God walketh in the midst of thy camp",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Rules for who may enter the congregation of the Lord, including Ammonites, Moabites, Edomites and Egyptians—The Lord turned Balaam’s curse into a blessing—Keep the camp clean, for the Lord walks in it—Do not return an escaped servant—Do not lend on usury to a brother—Pay thy vows—A traveler may eat from a neighbour’s vineyard or field but not carry away.",
 bg:["Balaam’s story is in Numbers 22–24. Even so, Ruth the Moabitess joined Israel and became an ancestor of King David and of Jesus (Ruth 4:13–17; Matthew 1:5–6).","Jesus’ disciples plucked ears of corn as they walked through a field, as 23:25 allowed; the Pharisees objected only because it was the Sabbath (Matthew 12:1–2)."]},
"deu.24":{title:"Remember that thou wast a bondman in Egypt",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Laws on divorce and remarriage—A newly married man stays home a year to cheer his wife—Do not take a millstone in pledge or keep a poor man’s garment overnight—Pay the hired servant before the sun goes down—Each person is put to death for his own sin—Leave the forgotten sheaf and the gleanings for the stranger, the fatherless and the widow.",
 bg:["Jesus said Moses allowed divorce “because of the hardness of your hearts: but from the beginning it was not so” (Matthew 19:8).","Ruth gleaned in Boaz’s field under the law of 24:19 (Ruth 2:2–3). King Amaziah obeyed 24:16 by not punishing the children of his father’s murderers (2 Kings 14:6)."]},
"deu.25":{title:"Thou shalt not muzzle the ox",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"A guilty man may be beaten no more than forty stripes—Thou shalt not muzzle the ox when he treadeth out the corn—A man is to raise up children for his brother who died childless, or have his shoe loosed—Have a perfect and just weight and measure—Remember what Amalek did to the feeble on the way.",
 bg:["Paul quoted 25:4 to teach that those who labor in the gospel may be supported (1 Corinthians 9:9; 1 Timothy 5:18). He also said he was beaten with “forty stripes save one” five times (2 Corinthians 11:24).","Marrying a brother’s widow is called levirate marriage. Boaz took off his shoe in a related custom when he redeemed Ruth (Ruth 4:7–10), and the Sadducees asked Jesus about this law (Matthew 22:23–28)."]},
"deu.26":{title:"A Syrian ready to perish was my father",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Bring the firstfruits in a basket to the priest and recite how the Lord brought Israel from Egypt into a land flowing with milk and honey—Rejoice in every good thing—In the third year declare that the tithe has been given to the Levite, stranger, fatherless and widow—Israel avouches the Lord to be their God, and he avouches them to be his peculiar people.",
 bg:["The “Syrian ready to perish” (26:5) is usually understood as Jacob, who lived many years in Haran in Syria with Laban. This short confession of Israel’s story is still recited in the Jewish Passover service.","“Avouched” (26:17–18) means declared or affirmed. Both sides make promises, which is what a covenant is."]},
"deu.27":{title:"And all the people shall say, Amen",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"After crossing Jordan, Israel is to set up great stones plastered and written with the law on mount Ebal and build an altar of whole stones—Six tribes are to stand on mount Gerizim to bless and six on mount Ebal to curse—The Levites pronounce twelve curses on hidden sins—To each, all the people answer, “Amen.”",
 bg:["Joshua carried out these instructions after entering the land, reading all the law before the people between the two mountains (Joshua 8:30–35). The town of Shechem lies in the valley between Gerizim and Ebal.","Many of the curses describe sins done in secret, such as moving a landmark or smiting a neighbour secretly. By saying “Amen,” each person agreed to the covenant."]},
"deu.28":{title:"The head, and not the tail",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"If Israel hearkens, they will be blessed in the city and in the field, in their basket and store—The Lord will make them “the head, and not the tail”—If they do not hearken, curses will come: disease, drought, defeat and siege—The Lord will scatter them “from the one end of the earth even unto the other”—They will find no ease among the nations.",
 bg:["This chapter, with chapter 27, records the blessings and cursings to be declared at Gerizim and Ebal. Leviticus 26 contains a similar list.","Later scripture shows these warnings coming true, including the terrible famine in besieged Samaria (2 Kings 6:28–29) and the scattering of Israel and Judah. The Book of Mormon teaches that the Lord will also gather scattered Israel again (1 Nephi 22:3–12)."]},
"deu.29":{title:"The secret things belong unto the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses makes a covenant with Israel in the land of Moab beside the covenant at Horeb—Their clothes and shoes waxed not old in forty years—All Israel, from captains to the water drawer, enters the covenant, along with those not yet born—One who blesses himself in his heart while disobeying will not be spared—The secret things belong to God, but the revealed things to us and our children.",
 bg:["Verse 15 says the covenant was made also “with him that is not here with us this day,” meaning future generations. Each new generation must choose the covenant for itself.","Verse 29 teaches that God has not revealed everything, but what he has revealed is given “that we may do all the words of this law.”"]},
"deu.30":{title:"Choose life",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"When scattered Israel returns to the Lord, he will gather them from all nations and circumcise their hearts—The commandment is not hidden or far off, but “very nigh unto thee, in thy mouth, and in thy heart”—I have set before thee life and good, and death and evil—Therefore choose life.",
 bg:["Paul quoted 30:11–14 to teach that the word of faith is near us, in our mouth and heart (Romans 10:6–8).","“Choose life, that both thou and thy seed may live” (30:19) teaches agency. Lehi taught that people “are free to choose liberty and eternal life, through the great Mediator of all men, or to choose captivity and death” (2 Nephi 2:27)."]},
"deu.31":{title:"Be strong and of a good courage",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"At 120 years old Moses tells Israel the Lord will go before them—He charges Joshua, “Be strong and of a good courage”—The law is to be read to all the people every seven years at the feast of tabernacles—The Lord foretells Israel’s rebellion and gives Moses a song as a witness—The book of the law is placed beside the ark.",
 bg:["The Lord repeated “be strong and of a good courage” to Joshua after Moses died (Joshua 1:6–9). Hebrews 13:5 echoes 31:6: “I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee.”","The law was to be read to men, women, children and strangers (31:12). Ezra read the law to all the people at a feast of tabernacles after the return from Babylon (Nehemiah 8:2–3, 14–18)."]},
"deu.32":{title:"He is the Rock, his work is perfect",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses’ song: his doctrine shall drop as the rain—He is the Rock, a God of truth—The Lord found Jacob in the wilderness and bore him as an eagle bears her young—Jeshurun waxed fat and forsook God—“To me belongeth vengeance”—Moses is told to climb mount Nebo, see the land and die.",
 bg:["Paul quoted 32:35 when he wrote, “Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord” (Romans 12:19; Hebrews 10:30). Jeshurun is a poetic name for Israel meaning “upright one.”","Calling God “the Rock” is common in scripture. Helaman taught his sons to build on “the rock of our Redeemer, who is Christ” (Helaman 5:12)."]},
"deu.33":{title:"Underneath are the everlasting arms",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses, the man of God, blesses the tribes of Israel before his death—Levi is to teach Israel the law and keep the Urim and Thummim—Joseph is blessed with the precious things of heaven and earth, and with them he shall push the people together to the ends of the earth—“The eternal God is thy refuge, and underneath are the everlasting arms.”",
 bg:["Moses’ blessings can be compared with Jacob’s blessings on his sons in Genesis 49. The tribe of Simeon is not named here.","The Lord used the language of Joseph’s blessing (33:17) about the latter-day gathering: “they shall push the people together from the ends of the earth” (D&C 58:45)."]},
"deu.34":{title:"No man knoweth of his sepulchre",year:"Date uncertain (about 1450 or 1250 BC)",
 sum:"Moses climbs mount Nebo and the Lord shows him all the promised land—Moses dies in Moab at 120 years, his eye not dim, and no man knows his sepulchre—Israel weeps for him thirty days—Joshua is full of the spirit of wisdom—There arose not a prophet since like unto Moses, whom the Lord knew face to face.",
 bg:["The Book of Mormon notes that “the scriptures saith the Lord took Moses unto himself,” and Latter-day Saints understand that Moses was translated (Alma 45:19). This allowed him to appear with Elijah on the Mount of Transfiguration (Matthew 17:3).","Moses appeared again in the Kirtland Temple in 1836 and gave Joseph Smith and Oliver Cowdery the keys of the gathering of Israel (D&C 110:11)."]},
});
